  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis approach employs a hybrid genetic algorithm with adaptive mutation and elitism to escape local optima and explore the non-convex solution space effectively. The core idea is to iteratively evolve a population of candidate step functions, leveraging combinatorial diversity from crossover and random perturbations to find a configuration with minimal maximum overlap integral. This method addresses the bottleneck of gradient-based approaches by maintaining global exploration through population-based stochastic search, while ensuring constraint feasibility through periodic rescaling.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: h is a numpy array of length n_points with entries h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = n_points / 2. The objective function is defined as:\n\nC5 = max_k (2.0 / n_points) * sum_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h[i] * (1 - h[(i + k) % n_points])\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nOperations:\n- For each candidate, compute all lags k \u2208 [0, 2n_points - 2] using np.correlate with mode=\"full\"\n- Apply elitism to retain top-performing candidates\n- Use uniform crossover and Gaussian mutation with clipping and rescaling to maintain constraints\n- Repair infeasible candidates by clamping to [0,1] followed by rescaling to maintain the sum constraint\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInputs: initial_h_values (if available), n_points, seed, budget_s\n\nStep 1: Parent validation\n- If initial_h_values is provided, scale to [0,1] using h = (h - np.min(h)) / (np.max(h) - np.min(h))\n- Clip out-of-bound values and normalize sum to exact value: \n   h = h / np.sum(h) * (n_points / 2)\n   h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n   h = h / np.sum(h) * (n_points / 2)\n\nStep 2: Population initialization\n- Set n_points = 512 (if not already provided)\n- Create initial population of 50 candidates:\n   a. Use initial_h_values as base\n   b. Generate 49 candidates by adding random noise (\u00b10.1) to the base, then:\n      i. Clip values to [0,1]\n      ii. Compute sum and rescale to n_points/2\n      iii. Apply jittering: add small random perturbations (\u00b10.05) to each element\n\nStep 3: Iterative evolution\nFor generation in 0 to 1000:\n   a. Evaluate all candidates in population\n   b. Track best candidate (lowest C5)\n   c. Select top 20 candidates for breeding\n   d. Generate 10 offspring:\n      i. For crossover: perform uniform crossover between two parents\n      ii. For mutation: apply Gaussian noise (std=0.02) to each element, then:\n         - Clip to [0,1]\n         - Compute sum and rescale to n_points/2\n      iii. Add offspring to new population\n   e. Replace old population with new population and top 10 elites\n   f. If 3 subsequent generations show <1e-4 improvement, reduce mutation std by 0.3\n\nStep 4: Termination and validation\n- After 1000 generations or time budget exhaustion\n- Re-evaluate the best candidate using the exact evaluation metric\n- Final checks:\n   - Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Confirm sum(h) == n_points / 2\n   - Recompute C5 to validate\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- Population size: 50\n- Initial mutation standard deviation: 0.02\n- Crossover rate: 1.0\n- Elite count: 10\n- Max generations: 1000\n- Early termination threshold: 1e-4\n\nVariations:\n- When improvement stagnates, increase population size by 20% and reduce mutation std\n- Use tournament selection (size=5) instead of uniform crossover\n- When parent is unavailable, generate 100 random initial candidates with uniform distribution\n- If C5 plateaus, switch to elite-only selection for 20 generations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant costs:\n- 1000 generations * (50 candidates * O(n) evaluation) = 500,000 operations\n- CPU estimate: ~150 seconds for n_points=512\n- Remaining time allows for 3 multistart runs or higher resolution\n\nRobustness handling:\n- For invalid proposals:\n   - Apply clipping to [0,1]\n   - Compute sum and rescale to n_points / 2\n- On solver failure, restore best candidate from memory\n- If any h[i] < 0 or >1, apply clipping and rescaling\n- Use np.isclose with atol=1e-6 for sum verification\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- np.all(0 <= h <= 1)\n- np.isclose(np.sum(h), n_points / 2, atol=1e-6)\n\nObjective consistency:\n- Recompute C5 as np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")) * dx\n\nOutput contract:\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- h_values is a numpy array of length 512\n- c5_bound is the computed maximum overlap integral\n- n_points is set to 512\n\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm np.min(h) >= 0 and np.max(h) <= 1\n- Validate that dx = 2.0 / 512\n- Ensure no element exceeds [0,1] boundaries",
